/* Generated. Section index for the sidebar search in docs.js. */
window.__DOCS_INDEX__ = [{"href":"index.html","title":"Overview","group":"Get started","text":"Stream every CDN request to Obsero so AI agent traffic can be classified on request headers. Reference implementations for AWS CloudFront and Google Cloud. Stream CDN request logs to Obsero Every HTTP request hitting your CDN becomes one POST to Obsero, so AI agent traffic can be classified on the request headers it actually sent. Two working end-to-end implementations \u2014 AWS CloudFront and Google Cloud \u2014 plus a demo site and a traffic harness for each. This is a starting point, not a product. Fork it, keep the parts that match your stack, throw away the rest. Quickstart \u2192 One command, four steps: a mock site, the pipeline, connect it, tear it down. The contract \u2192 The URL and the payload. The only thing you have to get right. AWS \u2192 CloudFront \u2192 Firehose \u2192 Lambda adapter. One real decision to make. Google Cloud \u2192 LB request logs \u2192 Pub/Sub \u2192 Cloud Run adapter. Named headers on the cheap path. <h2","sections":[{"id":"pick-your-path","title":"Pick your path","text":"id=\"pick-your-path\"> # Pick your path The same repo serves three kinds of reader. Start from the row that sounds like you. You are Do this Time New to this \u2014 you want to see it work before you touch anything real Follow the quickstart checklist . Every step says what to run and what you should see. setup.sh does the work; nothing needs editing. ~20 min, including the CDN deploy Already running a site on CloudFront \u2014 you want your real traffic Skip the mock site : deploy the pipeline, then pick your distribution from the list. Your distribution is never modified. ~5 min Experienced with Terraform \u2014 you want the module in your own stack Read one module block and what it creates , then the code itself: aws/ingestion/main.tf and the adapter, aws/ingestion/lambda/adapter/index.mjs . Or skip the infrastructure entirely and call sendEvent from wherever you already see requests. an afternoon to review A coding agent , or working with one Read AGENTS.md in the repo, or llms.txt for these docs as Markdown. More below . \u2014 Every file path on these pages links to that file on GitHub, and each page ends with the code it describes \u2014 so you can check any claim here against the source. <h2"},{"id":"the-shape-of-it","title":"The shape of it","text":"id=\"the-shape-of-it\"> # The shape of it Obsero cares about exactly two things: the URL you POST to, and the payload. Everything else \u2014 which cloud, which log source, which language, whether you batch, whether you use Terraform at all \u2014 is your call. one event per HTTP request POST https://analytics-staging.obsero.ai/v1/events content-type: application/json x-obsero-domain: acme.com # which site this event belongs to x-obsero-key: <tracking ID> # proves you own it { \"path\": \"/pricing\", \"method\": \"GET\", \"statusCode\": 200, \"headers\": { \"host\": \"acme.com\", \"user-agent\": \"Mozilla/5.0 ...; compatible; ChatGPT-User/1.0; +https://openai.com/bot\", \"accept\": \"*/*\", \"signature-agent\": \"\\\"https://chatgpt.com\\\"\" } } That is the whole integration. Read the contract in full \u2192 <h2"},{"id":"which-one-do-i-want","title":"Which one do I want?","text":"id=\"which-one-do-i-want\"> # Which one do I want? Your setup Start here Why CloudFront in front of anything AWS Two log sources: cheap (User-Agent only) or full headers for ~$11/mo. Google Cloud load balancer + Cloud CDN GCP Full headers on the cheap path \u2014 no trade to make. A plain GCS bucket behind Cloud CDN GCP, caveat first Backend buckets emit no request logs at all. Your origin has to be a backend service . Cloudflare, Fastly, Akamai, a bare origin none of them Import sendEvent and call it from wherever you already see requests. That is the entire integration. <h2"},{"id":"whats-in-the-repo","title":"What's in the repo","text":"id=\"whats-in-the-repo\"> # What's in the repo obsero.mjs the contract: build an event, validate it, POST it setup.sh the interactive installer destroy.sh teardown, plus a sweep for resources Terraform lost track of aws/ CloudFront -> Firehose -> Lambda adapter -> Obsero gcp/ LB request logs -> Pub/Sub -> Cloud Run adapter -> Obsero docs/ this site Each cloud directory has the same three parts: ingestion/ the Terraform module you would actually ship site/ a demo site to point it at, so you can see traffic flow test/ a traffic harness: 19 client personas, then score what arrived Both ingestion/ modules deploy on their own. site/ and test/ exist to prove the thing works and are the first things to delete once it does. <h2"},{"id":"agents","title":"If you work with a coding agent","text":"id=\"agents\"> # If you work with a coding agent There are AGENTS.md files at the repo root and in each cloud directory. They carry the contract, the setup order, the failure modes that look like bugs but are not, and the handful of things in the Terraform that must not be \u201ccleaned up\u201d because they fail silently when removed. Point your agent at them before it touches anything. File Read by What is in it AGENTS.md any agent working in the repo the contract, the menu and its flags, the order to debug in, the rules CLAUDE.md Claude Code, which looks for this name imports AGENTS.md , so there is one source of truth aws/AGENTS.md , gcp/AGENTS.md an agent working on one cloud symptom \u2192 cause \u2192 fix, and what each step of setup.sh applies llms.txt an agent reading these docs over the web an index of every page as Markdown, and the key source files llms-full.txt an agent that wants it all in one fetch every page of these docs in one Markdown file Every page is also published as Markdown next to its HTML \u2014 quickstart.md beside quickstart.html \u2014 and linked from the View as Markdown link at the bottom of the page. Agents that do the driving themselves can use the non-interactive forms: ./setup.sh aws pipeline --token <tracking-id> ./setup.sh aws connect --dist <distribution-id> ./setup.sh aws status The rule in AGENTS.md that matters most: an agent must never run an apply or a destroy without the developer's go-ahead on that specific command. These stacks cost real money."}]},{"href":"quickstart.html","title":"Quickstart","group":"Get started","text":"Step by step: deploy a mock site, deploy the ingestion pipeline with your tracking ID, connect it to your CloudFront distributions, then tear it all down. Quickstart One command. Deploy a mock site, build a real ingestion pipeline, connect the two, watch classified traffic arrive, then tear it all down. Or skip the mock site and connect a site you already run. This costs real money The demo rigs bill while they are up. The GCP forwarding rule is about $18/month whether or not any traffic hits it . Run step 4 when you are done \u2014 not tomorrow. <h2","sections":[{"id":"before-you-start","title":"Before you start","text":"id=\"before-you-start\"> # Before you start You need Both AWS GCP Terraform \u2265 1.5 \u2713 Node \u2265 18 \u2713 Authenticated CLI aws configure gcloud auth login Docker \u2014 \u2713 images are built locally A tracking ID from Obsero \u2713 setup.sh checks all of this for you and tells you exactly what is missing before it creates anything. On GCP it also checks the required APIs and offers to enable them \u2014 but not the IAM roles, which are the thing most likely to bite. Check those first \u2192 <h2"},{"id":"checklist","title":"Your checklist","text":"id=\"checklist\"> # Your checklist Everything you do, in order, for AWS. Each item says what to run and what you should see; if you see something else, stop there \u2014 the item after it will not work either. The steps in detail explains what each one builds. Install the tools terraform -version # v1.5 or newer node --version # v18 or newer aws --version # any v2 Missing one? setup.sh names it and links the installer, so you can also just run it and see. Log the AWS CLI in aws sts get-caller-identity You should see your account number and an ARN. An error means the CLI has no credentials: run aws configure , or export AWS_PROFILE=<name> if you use named profiles. Everything below runs as this identity, in this account. Get the code git clone https://github.com/Obsero-ai/obsero-public.git cd obsero-public Check your tracking ID The site token Obsero issued for your domain. It is sent as x-obsero-key on every event and is what proves the domain is yours. Check it before building anything \u2014 a rejected ID makes the pipeline forward into nothing, silently: node obsero.mjs --token <tracking-id> --domain <your-domain> You should see the event accepted. Then open the menu \u2014 everything from here is a number typed into it: ./setup.sh aws Press 1 \u2014 deploy the mock site Takes 3\u20135 minutes. You should see Site is up and a https://\u2026.cloudfront.net URL. Open it: a small site with a few pages. Skip this item if you are connecting your own site . Press 2 \u2014 deploy the pipeline Paste your tracking ID and press Enter; each character shows as * , then it confirms the first and last four. Answer y to the test event. You should see Obsero accepted it , then, 2\u20133 minutes later, Pipeline is live with connected: nothing yet . That is expected \u2014 the next item connects it. Press 3 \u2014 connect A numbered list of every CloudFront distribution in the account. Type the number next to the mock site , press Enter to see it ticked, press Enter on an empty line, then y . You should see Connected and the distribution ID. Press 5 \u2014 send traffic and score it Sends requests as 19 client personas, waits about 90 seconds for delivery, then scores how many reached Obsero with their headers intact. You should see every request correlated and no header loss. Some missing right after connecting is the 60\u201390s latency floor: run it again. Press 4 when you are finished Type destroy to confirm. It removes everything setup.sh created and then checks nothing is left billing. Distributions it did not create are only disconnected, never deleted. Press 6 at any point to see what is deployed and what is connected. <h2"},{"id":"done-when","title":"You're done when","text":"id=\"done-when\"> # You're done when ./setup.sh aws status shows the pipeline deployed and at least one distribution connected . make -C aws logs shows the adapter forwarding events within 90 seconds of a request to a connected site. The traffic score correlates every request \u2014 the measured baseline is 78 of 78 on AWS, with zero header loss. Events show up in Obsero under your domain, with AI agents and crawlers told apart from browsers. Stuck on one of these? Troubleshooting has the order to check things in. <h2"},{"id":"your-own-site","title":"Use your own site instead of the mock","text":"id=\"your-own-site\"> # Use your own site instead of the mock The mock site is only there so you have traffic to look at. If you already run a site on CloudFront, connect that instead \u2014 the pipeline is the same either way. ./setup.sh aws # then: 2 # the pipeline, with your tracking ID 3 # tick your distribution, listed by domain Or, without the menu: ./setup.sh aws pipeline --token <tracking-id> ./setup.sh aws connect --dist <distribution-id> What happens to your distribution: nothing Connecting never edits the distribution's configuration. It adds a CloudFront standard logging v2 delivery that copies each request's log record into the pipeline's Firehose. Traffic, caching and latency are untouched. If the distribution already uses standard logging v2 \u2014 to S3, say \u2014 that keeps working: its existing logging source is reused, and left in place when you disconnect. Which headers arrive. Standard logging carries User-Agent, Referer, Host and X-Forwarded-For \u2014 enough to tell most agents apart, not enough to verify Web Bot Auth signatures. The one real decision explains the trade. What it costs. No idle cost; Firehose bills per GB, which is cents at typical volumes. Cost \u2192 Permissions. Your AWS identity needs to list CloudFront distributions and create CloudWatch Logs deliveries, in us-east-1 \u2014 where CloudFront logging always lives. Stopping. Run 3 again and untick it, or ./setup.sh aws connect --dist none . Destroying the pipeline disconnects everything too. <h2"},{"id":"zero-cost-first","title":"Two checks that cost nothing","text":"id=\"zero-cost-first\"> # Two checks that cost nothing Both of these pass or fail in seconds and rule out most of what goes wrong later. Run them before you create any infrastructure. cd aws # or gcp make test # adapter payload vs the contract \u2014 no cloud account needed make event TOKEN=<tracking-id> DOMAIN=acme.com make test feeds sample log lines through the real adapter parser and asserts the result against validateEvent . make event POSTs one hand-made event, so you know your tracking ID is accepted before anything depends on it. <h2"},{"id":"the-steps","title":"The steps in detail","text":"id=\"the-steps\"> # The steps in detail ./setup.sh It asks which cloud, then shows this: Obsero ingestion -- aws 1) Deploy the mock site a site worth collecting logs from 2) Deploy the ingestion pipeline asks for your tracking ID 3) Connect pick distributions already in your account 4) Destroy everything the rig bills while it is up 5) Send mock traffic and score what arrived 6) Status -- what is deployed right now 7) Switch cloud q) Quit Deploy the mock site A static site with real pages, behind a real CDN, so there is traffic worth logging. On AWS that is a private S3 bucket behind CloudFront; on GCP it is the same pages served from Cloud Run behind a global external Application Load Balancer with Cloud CDN in front. Nothing is sent to Obsero at this point, and no tracking ID is needed. The step prints the site URL when it finishes \u2014 open it. ==> Site is up https://d1234abcd.cloudfront.net Open it. Nothing is being sent to Obsero yet -- step 2 builds the pipeline, step 3 connects this site (or any other distribution) to it. Budget 3\u20135 minutes on AWS (CloudFront is the slow part) and 5\u20138 on GCP, which also builds a container image on the way. Deploy the ingestion pipeline This is the step that needs your tracking ID \u2014 the site token Obsero issued you, sent as the x-obsero-key header on every forwarded event. Each character shows as * so you can see a paste landed, and the first and last four are echoed back to check. It is saved to <cloud>/site/terraform/terraform.tfvars , which is gitignored and chmod 600 , because terraform destroy and every later apply need it too. ==> Your Obsero tracking ID The site token Obsero issued you. It is sent as the x-obsero-key header on every forwarded event, and is what proves the domain is yours. Tracking ID (paste, then Enter): ******************************** got 32 characters: wuJ7...oF_E v using wuJ7...oF_E ==> Collecting from nothing yet -- the pipeline is built idle, and step 3 connects it to any CloudFront distribution in your account, the mock site included Send one test event first, to check the ID is accepted? [y/N] Say yes. A rejected tracking ID means the pipeline forwards into nothing, silently \u2014 better to find out in one second than after a ten-minute apply. Then the pipeline goes up: on AWS, CloudFront standard logs into Firehose into an adapter Lambda; on GCP, load balancer request logs through a Log Router sink into Pub/Sub into a Cloud Run adapter. On AWS the pipeline does not need the mock site and is built collecting from nothing; step 3 decides where it collects from. On GCP it attaches to the mock site's backend service here, and builds the site too if step 1 has not run. Give it a minute before you judge it AWS: first events land in 60\u201390 seconds. CloudFront flushes continuously but Firehose buffers for at least 60s, and that is a floor, not a setting. GCP: the header allow-list takes several minutes to reach the edge. Events that arrive headerless right after an apply are propagation, not breakage. Connect (AWS) Lists every CloudFront distribution in your account \u2014 the mock site and anything you already run \u2014 and lets you tick the ones the pipeline collects from. Untick one to disconnect it. ==> CloudFront distributions in this account 1) [x] E1A2B3C4D5E6F7 d1234abcd.cloudfront.net the mock site 2) [ ] E9Z8Y7X6W5V4U3 acme.com,www.acme.com reuses its existing logging source \"CreatedByCloudFront-E9Z8Y7X6W5V4U3\" numbers toggle (e.g. \"1 3\"), a = all, n = none, Enter = done, q = cancel Connect: 2 Your distributions are never modified. Connecting adds a CloudFront standard logging v2 delivery from the distribution into the pipeline's Firehose. A distribution can have only one standard logging v2 source, so if it already has one \u2014 the CloudFront console creates it the first time anyone turns that logging on \u2014 it is reused and left in place on disconnect. The picks are saved as connected_distribution_arns in terraform.tfvars , so every later apply keeps them. Destroy everything Terraform does the work; the wrapper handles what it cannot \u2014 emptying S3 buckets whose contents Terraform does not own, passing each stack the credentials it needs, and then checking the cloud afterwards for anything still standing. A lost state file or a half-finished destroy leaves resources that no terraform plan will ever mention. ./destroy.sh --check # just tell me what is still deployed ./destroy.sh --dry-run # show the plan, touch nothing ./destroy.sh aws # one cloud ./destroy.sh --sweep # delete leftovers too, not just report them CloudFront is the one exception: a distribution has to be disabled, left to propagate, and deleted minutes later, so the script reports it rather than pretending it can be done in one pass. <h2"},{"id":"watch-it-work","title":"Watch it work","text":"id=\"watch-it-work\"> # Watch it work Option 5 sends a reproducible mix of traffic as 19 client personas \u2014 each carrying the headers that client really sends \u2014 waits out the delivery latency, then scores what actually arrived. ./setup.sh aws traffic -n 40 Every request is tagged ?mt=<runId>-<seq> . The scorer reads the records back, replays them through the real adapter parser, correlates each tag, and diffs headers sent against headers received. Measured baselines: 78/78 correlated on AWS, 40/40 on GCP, zero header loss on either. kind personas ai-agent ChatGPT-User, Claude-User, Perplexity-User, OAI-SearchBot, DuckAssistBot ai-crawler GPTBot, ClaudeBot, PerplexityBot, Meta-ExternalAgent, Bytespider, CCBot, Amazonbot human Chrome/Windows, Safari/iPhone, Firefox/macOS, Chrome/Android search-bot Googlebot, Bingbot, Applebot cd aws make traffic N=40 # weighted mix make traffic N=20 KIND=ai-agent # one class make traffic N=10 ONLY=gptbot SEED=7 # specific, reproducible make personas # list them <h2"},{"id":"non-interactive","title":"Without the menu","text":"id=\"non-interactive\"> # Without the menu Every menu item has a flag form, for CI or for driving it from a script: ./setup.sh aws site ./setup.sh aws pipeline --token <tracking-id> ./setup.sh aws connect --dist E1A2B3C4D5E6F7 # exactly these; --dist none clears ./setup.sh aws connect --dist +E9Z8Y7X6W5V4U3 # add one to what is connected ./setup.sh aws traffic -n 40 ./setup.sh aws status ./setup.sh aws destroy --yes Or skip setup.sh entirely and use the make targets directly \u2014 make with no argument in either cloud directory lists them all. make deploy is steps 1\u20133 for the mock site in one go; make e2e is deploy, traffic, wait, score. <h2"},{"id":"what-setup-does","title":"What setup.sh is actually doing","text":"id=\"what-setup-does\"> # What setup.sh is actually doing Worth knowing, because it explains the one thing that looks odd: the site, the pipeline and its connections are one Terraform stack , not three. Step 1 is a targeted apply of the site resources only. On AWS, step 2 is a targeted apply of the module.ingestion block, which does not reference the site, and step 3 re-applies that same module with a new connected_distribution_arns list. On GCP, step 2 is the plain untargeted apply. Because both stacks declare obsero_site_token with no default, step 1 writes the placeholder set-in-step-2 into terraform.tfvars so it can plan at all. Step 2 refuses to apply while that is still the value."}]},{"href":"contract.html","title":"The contract","group":"Reference","text":"The URL you POST to and the payload Obsero expects. The only thing you have to get right. The contract The URL you POST to, and the payload. Everything else is yours to decide. Which cloud, which log source, which language, whether you buffer or send per request, whether you use Terraform at all \u2014 none of that is part of the contract. This page is. <h2","sections":[{"id":"the-request","title":"The request","text":"id=\"the-request\"> # The request one event per HTTP request POST https://analytics-staging.obsero.ai/v1/events content-type: application/json x-obsero-domain: acme.com # which site this event belongs to x-obsero-key: <tracking ID> # proves you own it { \"path\": \"/pricing\", \"method\": \"GET\", \"statusCode\": 200, \"headers\": { \"host\": \"acme.com\", \"user-agent\": \"Mozilla/5.0 ...; compatible; ChatGPT-User/1.0; +https://openai.com/bot\", \"accept\": \"*/*\", \"signature-agent\": \"\\\"https://chatgpt.com\\\"\", \"signature-input\": \"sig1=(\\\"@authority\\\" \\\"signature-agent\\\");created=...\", \"signature\": \"sig1=:...:\" } } field rules path Path only . /pricing , never /pricing?ref=x and never a full URL. Leaving the query string on makes every distinct URL its own page in the analytics \u2014 the most common mistake by far. method Uppercase. \"GET\" . statusCode A number, not a string. 200 . headers Lowercase names \u2192 verbatim values. This is what classification runs on, so send as many as you can get. <h2"},{"id":"headers","title":"Headers worth carrying","text":"id=\"headers\"> # Headers worth carrying Roughly in order of value: header what it buys you user-agent everything starts here from GPTBot, Googlebot signature-agent , signature-input , signature Web Bot Auth \u2014 proves an agent is who it claims to be, rather than taking a User-Agent at face value accept , accept-language browsers vs */* bots sec-ch-ua , sec-ch-ua-platform , sec-ch-ua-mobile client hints \u2014 browsers only sec-fetch-dest , sec-fetch-mode , sec-fetch-site presence alone is the signal Never forward these authorization , cookie , set-cookie . Both adapters strip them before anything leaves your account. Keep that behaviour if you rewrite one. <h2"},{"id":"obsero-mjs","title":"obsero.mjs \u2014 the whole contract in one file","text":"id=\"obsero-mjs\"> # obsero.mjs \u2014 the whole contract in one file Dependency-free, about 150 lines: buildEvent , sendEvent , validateEvent . Run it to send a test event and confirm your tracking ID works before you deploy anything: node obsero.mjs --token <tracking-id> --domain acme.com Then, in your own code, this is genuinely all it takes: the entire integration import { buildEvent, sendEvent } from \"./obsero.mjs\"; await sendEvent( buildEvent({ path: req.url, method: req.method, statusCode: res.statusCode, headers: req.headers, }), { token: process.env.OBSERO_SITE_TOKEN, domain: \"acme.com\" }, ); If you can call that from your edge middleware, your origin server or your log processor, you are done. You do not need anything else in this repo \u2014 and if you are on Cloudflare, Fastly, Akamai or a bare origin, this is the integration. Do not port a cloud directory you do not need. buildEvent does the two things that are easy to get wrong for you: it strips the query string from path (and tolerates being handed an absolute URL), and it lowercases, trims and filters the headers. <h2"},{"id":"validate","title":"Check it before you ship it","text":"id=\"validate\"> # Check it before you ship it validateEvent is the machine-checkable form of everything on this page. It returns a list of problems, empty when the payload is good: cd aws # or gcp make test That runs the real adapter parser over sample log lines and checks each resulting payload against validateEvent . No cloud account needed. If it passes, Obsero will accept what you produce. <h2"},{"id":"adapting","title":"Adapting an adapter","text":"id=\"adapting\"> # Adapting an adapter Both adapters have the same three-part shape, and only one part is the contract: 1. THE CONTRACT build the payload and POST it <- keep this 2. LOG PARSING log line/entry -> event <- yours to rewrite 3. TRANSPORT unwrap the batch, ack it <- yours to rewrite aws/ingestion/lambda/adapter/index.mjs gcp/ingestion/adapter/index.mjs Change parts 2 and 3 to match your log source, leave part 1 alone, then run make test . Fixtures live in each cloud's test/adapter.test.mjs \u2014 add one for any new log shape rather than testing against live traffic. <h2"},{"id":"tracking-id","title":"Getting a tracking ID","text":"id=\"tracking-id\"> # Getting a tracking ID Ask Obsero for one, then keep it out of the repo. Both site/terraform/ directories read it from a gitignored terraform.tfvars : obsero_site_token = \"...\" setup.sh writes that file for you, chmod 600 . The deployed adapters read x-obsero-domain off each request's own Host header, so there is no domain to configure \u2014 only the token."}]},{"href":"aws.html","title":"AWS","group":"Deployment guides","text":"CloudFront to Firehose to a Lambda adapter. Choosing a log source, measured gotchas, and cost. AWS \u2014 CloudFront Streams every request hitting a CloudFront distribution to Obsero. viewer -> CloudFront -> Firehose -> adapter Lambda -> POST /v1/events \\-> S3 backup bucket (undeliverable batches) Everything runs in your own AWS account. The adapter strips authorization , cookie and set-cookie before anything leaves it. <h2","sections":[{"id":"try-it","title":"Try it without an AWS account","text":"id=\"try-it\"> # Try it without an AWS account cd aws make test # adapter payload vs the contract make event TOKEN=<tracking-id> DOMAIN=acme.com # POST one event, check the ID Then, to watch it work end to end, the quickstart \u2014 or the make targets directly: make deploy # demo site + pipeline, connected to each other make traffic N=40 # mock browser / AI-agent / crawler traffic make check # score what actually reached the endpoint make logs # follow the adapter forwarding, live make destroy # tear it all down again <h2"},{"id":"wiring-it-in","title":"Wiring it into your own distribution","text":"id=\"wiring-it-in\"> # Wiring it into your own distribution One module block. The module never touches your distribution \u2014 in standard mode it only needs the distribution's ARN, and in realtime mode it hands back an ARN for you to attach \u2014 so it works whether or not Terraform manages your CloudFront. standard mode \u2014 the whole integration module \"obsero_ingestion\" { source = \"./ingestion\" name_prefix = \"acme-prod\" site_token = var.obsero_site_token # CloudFront delivers straight into Firehose. No idle cost. log_source = \"standard\" distribution_arns = [ aws_cloudfront_distribution.site.arn, \"arn:aws:cloudfront::123456789012:distribution/E2ABC123\", # one Terraform does not manage ] } distribution_arns can be empty: the pipeline is then built idle, costing nothing, until you add one. If a distribution already has a standard logging v2 source \u2014 the CloudFront console creates one the first time anyone enables that logging \u2014 also pass it in existing_delivery_sources , because a distribution can only have one: existing_delivery_sources = { \"arn:aws:cloudfront::123456789012:distribution/E2ABC123\" = \"CreatedByCloudFront-E2ABC123\" } For realtime , drop distribution_arns and attach the exported ARN to each cache behaviour you want logged instead: default_cache_behavior { # ... realtime_log_config_arn = module.obsero_ingestion.realtime_log_config_arn } Behaviours without the ARN emit nothing, so you can roll it out one path pattern at a time. See ingestion/examples/ for both shapes and ingestion/README.md for every input. <h2"},{"id":"connect","title":"Connecting distributions you already run","text":"id=\"connect\"> # Connecting distributions you already run With setup.sh , this is step 3: it lists every distribution in the account, detects existing logging sources for you, and writes both variables above into aws/site/terraform/terraform.tfvars . See using your own site for the walkthrough. ./setup.sh aws connect # pick from a list ./setup.sh aws connect --dist E2ABC123,E3DEF456 # exactly these ./setup.sh aws connect --dist +E2ABC123 # add one ./setup.sh aws connect --dist none # disconnect all To check what is connected from the AWS side, independent of Terraform state: aws logs describe-deliveries --region us-east-1 \\ --query 'deliveries[].[deliverySourceName,deliveryDestinationArn]' --output table aws logs describe-delivery-sources --region us-east-1 \\ --query 'deliverySources[].[name,resourceArns[0]]' --output table Each connected distribution has one delivery whose destination ends in <name_prefix>-firehose . Disconnecting deletes that delivery, and the source too if the module created it; a source that was there first is left alone. <h2"},{"id":"what-it-creates","title":"What the module creates","text":"id=\"what-it-creates\"> # What the module creates For reviewing before you apply it in a real account. Everything is prefixed with name_prefix and tagged ManagedBy = obsero-ingestion . Source: aws/ingestion/main.tf and aws/ingestion/lambda.tf . Resource How many Why it exists aws_kinesis_firehose_delivery_stream.this 1 Buffers log records and POSTs batches to the adapter. Tagged LogDeliveryEnabled = true in standard mode \u2014 without it nothing is delivered . aws_lambda_function.adapter + aws_lambda_function_url.adapter 1 Turns each log record into one Obsero event and returns Firehose's ack. Why a Lambda at all . aws_s3_bucket.backup 1 Batches the endpoint never accepted, after retry_duration . Expires after backup_retention_days . aws_cloudwatch_log_delivery_destination.this 1 (standard) Points CloudFront standard logging at the Firehose. Shared by every connected distribution; free while idle. aws_cloudwatch_log_delivery_source.this 1 per connected distribution without one Declares the distribution's access logs as a source. Skipped for distributions in existing_delivery_sources . aws_cloudwatch_log_delivery.this 1 per connected distribution The connection itself: source \u2192 destination, with the fields in standard_log_fields . IAM roles, CloudWatch log groups 2 + 2 Firehose's role (backup bucket, logs, Kinesis in realtime) and the adapter's execution role; one log group each, kept log_retention_days . aws_kinesis_stream.this , aws_cloudfront_realtime_log_config.this realtime only The ~$11/month shard and the config you attach to cache behaviours. Bad inputs fail at plan time, not as empty events: terraform_data.validate_config checks the log field lists and that every existing_delivery_sources key is also connected. <h2"},{"id":"log-source","title":"Which log source? The one real decision","text":"id=\"log-source\"> # Which log source? The one real decision CloudFront makes you choose. standard default realtime Idle cost none ~$11/mo per Kinesis shard At 1M req/mo ~$0.15 ~$11 Latency ~1\u20132 min seconds Headers User-Agent, Referer, Host, X-Forwarded-For full viewer header set Web Bot Auth signatures no yes Client hints ( sec-ch-ua ) no yes Start on standard . Move to realtime when you want to verify an agent rather than take its User-Agent at face value: standard logging v2 has no cs-headers field, so signature-agent / signature-input / signature never arrive at all. That is not a tuning problem \u2014 it is a different log source. GCP has no such trade : it carries named headers on the cheap path. <h2"},{"id":"gotchas","title":"Before you trust the numbers","text":"id=\"gotchas\"> # Before you trust the numbers Four behaviours that are easy to mistake for bugs. All measured on a live deployment, all written up in aws/NOTES.md . Real-time logs are best-effort CloudFront delivers real-time logs on a best-effort basis; some requests are never written to Kinesis at all. Measured across five runs (188 requests) at sampling_rate = 100 : four delivered 100%, one delivered 20 of 30. The lost records correlated with nothing \u2014 not status code, path, client or latency \u2014 and Kinesis IncomingRecords confirmed they were never sent rather than lost downstream. Treat the counts as a high-fidelity sample, not an exact ledger. Do not reconcile them against origin logs or bill directly on them. Delivery is at-least-once If any event in a Firehose batch fails to forward, the adapter fails the whole batch so Firehose retries \u2014 which duplicates the events that already landed. After retry_duration the batch goes to the backup bucket rather than being dropped. Latency floor: 60\u201390s CloudFront flushes real-time logs continuously, but Firehose buffers for at least 60s. That is a floor, not a tuning knob. The LogDeliveryEnabled tag Standard logging v2 writes through the AWSServiceRoleForLogDelivery service-linked role, whose policy allows firehose:PutRecordBatch only on streams tagged LogDeliveryEnabled = true . Without the tag CloudFront delivers nothing , and there is no error anywhere \u2014 the delivery reports healthy, the Firehose reports healthy, and IncomingRecords simply stays at zero. The module applies the tag automatically. Worth knowing if you ever hand-build this. # Two CloudFront parsing behaviours (realtime only) Both are handled by the adapter. They are documented because they bite anyone reading the raw Kinesis stream themselves. Standard mode is self-describing JSON, so neither applies there. Field order is not your field order. CloudFront emits real-time log fields in its own canonical order, ignoring the order in the log config. Records are tab-separated with no header row, so a positional parser that trusts the config order reads every column shifted. The module sorts log_fields into canonical order and hands that same list to the adapter; log_fields_in_order exports it. cs-uri-stem includes the query string in real-time logs, unlike standard access logs where it is path-only. The adapter strips it, or every distinct query string would register as its own path in the analytics. <h2"},{"id":"why-lambda","title":"Why the adapter Lambda exists at all","text":"id=\"why-lambda\"> # Why the adapter Lambda exists at all Firehose's HTTP endpoint destination cannot post to /v1/events directly: it wraps records in {requestId, timestamp, records:[{data}]} it sends the key as X-Amz-Firehose-Access-Key , and cannot send arbitrary headers such as x-obsero-domain / x-obsero-key it requires a specific JSON ack back, or treats the delivery as failed The adapter unwraps the envelope, converts each log line into one Obsero event, strips excluded headers, and returns the ack. Its Function URL is unauthenticated at the AWS layer \u2014 Firehose cannot SigV4-sign to a Function URL \u2014 so the shared Firehose access key is what the handler checks. The URL is not secret, but is useless without that key. # Concurrency footprint The adapter uses one concurrent Lambda execution per Kinesis shard . Firehose reads each shard serially and invokes once per buffer window, so at the default kinesis_shard_count = 1 the steady state is exactly 1 concurrent execution, ~1s per invocation, one invocation per minute. Measured, not estimated. That matters on accounts with a low concurrency ceiling. New AWS accounts are capped at 10 concurrent executions account-wide , and at that ceiling reserved_concurrent_executions cannot be set at all \u2014 AWS requires unreserved concurrency to stay at or above 10, so any reservation is rejected. The adapter therefore shares an unprotected pool with every other function in the account. If another function saturates the pool, the Function URL returns 429, Firehose treats it as a failed delivery, retries for retry_duration , and then writes the batch to the backup bucket. Nothing is lost, but those events do not reach Obsero until the backup is replayed. Watch Throttles if you share a small pool. <h2"},{"id":"cost","title":"Cost","text":"id=\"cost\"> # Cost Lambda, CloudFront and S3 usage at demo volume sit inside the AWS free tier. Kinesis Data Streams does not have a free tier \u2014 one provisioned shard is about $11/month ($0.015/shard-hour) and is realtime mode's floor cost, since CloudFront real-time logs can only be delivered to Kinesis. Firehose adds ~$0.029/GB ingested, which is cents at typical volumes. In standard mode there is no Kinesis stream and therefore no idle cost at all; Firehose only reaches $11/month at roughly 380M requests. <h2"},{"id":"teardown","title":"Tearing it down","text":"id=\"teardown\"> # Tearing it down make destroy # terraform destroy, then check nothing is left standing make leftovers # just tell me what is still deployed Both call destroy.sh , which empties the S3 buckets first \u2014 Terraform refuses to delete a bucket holding objects it did not create, and that is precisely what the backup bucket collects. The module itself keeps backup_force_destroy = false , so a customer's terraform destroy will stop rather than silently discard events that never reached Obsero. The demo site sets it to true , because nobody is going to replay those."}]},{"href":"gcp.html","title":"GCP","group":"Deployment guides","text":"Load balancer request logs to Pub/Sub to a Cloud Run adapter. Named headers on the cheap path, permissions, and cost. Google Cloud Streams every request hitting a Cloud Load Balancing backend service to Obsero. viewer -> Cloud CDN + external Application LB -> Cloud Logging -> Log Router sink -> Pub/Sub -> Cloud Run adapter -> POST /v1/events \\-> dead-letter topic (replayable) Everything runs in your own project. The adapter strips authorization , cookie and set-cookie before anything leaves it. <h2","sections":[{"id":"backend-buckets","title":"Read this first","text":"id=\"backend-buckets\"> # Read this first Backend buckets produce no request logs BackendBucket has no logConfig field at all in the Compute v1 API, so a static site served straight from Cloud Storage through Cloud CDN emits nothing to ship. Your origin has to be a backend service . Apps on Cloud Run, GKE or a MIG already are one and are unaffected. A pure GCS origin has to change before it can be onboarded \u2014 which is why the demo site here serves its static pages from Cloud Run rather than a bucket. <h2"},{"id":"try-it","title":"Try it without a GCP project","text":"id=\"try-it\"> # Try it without a GCP project cd gcp make test # adapter payload vs the contract make event TOKEN=<tracking-id> DOMAIN=acme.com # POST one event, check the ID Then, to watch it work end to end, the quickstart \u2014 or the make targets directly: make site # site + LB + header logging only make sample # confirm the headers reach Cloud Logging make deploy # the rest: sink, Pub/Sub, adapter make traffic N=40 # mock browser / AI-agent / crawler traffic make check # score what actually reached the endpoint make destroy # the load balancer bills at idle -- do this The two-stage start is deliberate: make sample confirms the header allow-list behaves before you build anything downstream of it. <h2"},{"id":"wiring-it-in","title":"Wiring it into your own load balancer","text":"id=\"wiring-it-in\"> # Wiring it into your own load balancer the whole integration module \"obsero_ingestion\" { source = \"./ingestion\" project_id = var.project_id name_prefix = \"acme-prod\" site_token = var.obsero_site_token backend_service_name = google_compute_backend_service.site.name } Unlike the AWS module there is no ARN to attach \u2014 this one configures logging on the named backend service directly. Better DX, at the cost of mutating a resource you own. It is reversible; terraform destroy takes the allow-list back off. <h2"},{"id":"headers","title":"The headline win: named request headers on the cheap path","text":"id=\"headers\"> # The headline win: named request headers on the cheap path CloudFront forces a choice \u2014 pay ~$11/month for real-time logs to get the full header set, or take standard logging and lose everything but User-Agent. GCP has no such trade. BackendServiceLogConfig.loggingHttpRequestHeaders names the headers you want and they arrive through the ordinary, cheap path: from, # GPTBot, Googlebot signature-agent, signature-input, signature, # Web Bot Auth accept, accept-language, # browsers vs */* bots sec-ch-ua, sec-ch-ua-platform, sec-ch-ua-mobile, # client hints sec-fetch-dest # presence alone is the signal The API caps the list at 10 An eleventh header is rejected outright. Ten is enough only because user-agent , referer and the client IP arrive as first-class httpRequest fields whether or not you name them \u2014 spending slots on those would waste them. The adapter merges both sources. So the question is never \u201ccan I add one more?\u201d It is which one you are dropping. sec-fetch-mode and sec-fetch-site were the two cut from the default list. It is an allow-list, not \u201call headers\u201d, which is arguably the better default: the payload carries only what classification needs, so nothing incidental leaves your project. # What the logged headers actually look like The allow-list lands under jsonPayload.loggingHttpRequestHeaders , as an array of pairs , with every value base64-encoded : \"loggingHttpRequestHeaders\": [ { \"headerKey\": \"from\", \"headerValue\": \"Ym90QG9wZW5haS5jb20=\" }, { \"headerKey\": \"signature-agent\", \"headerValue\": \"Imh0dHBzOi8vY2hhdGdwdC5jb20i\" } ] The base64 is a feature. It is why Web Bot Auth signatures survive byte-for-byte \u2014 quotes, commas and embedded colons included. CloudFront's percent-encoded cs-headers needed careful splitting on %0A before decoding, or a header value could forge an extra header. Nothing here can. An absent header is simply missing from the array. There is no empty-string placeholder, so \u201cnot sent\u201d and \u201csent empty\u201d are indistinguishable. httpRequest.userAgent and httpRequest.remoteIp are populated regardless , confirming those slots are free. <h2"},{"id":"permissions","title":"Permissions \u2014 roles/editor is not enough","text":"id=\"permissions\"> # Permissions \u2014 roles/editor is not enough roles/editor deliberately excludes log-routing configuration and resource-level setIamPolicy , which are the core of this pipeline. Check these before anyone runs apply. Role For roles/editor load balancer, CDN, backend services, NEGs, Pub/Sub topics and subscriptions, Artifact Registry roles/logging.configWriter the Log Router sink, and the _Default exclusion that stops you paying to store logs you already routed. Not implied by Editor \u2014 apply fails at the sink. roles/pubsub.admin pubsub.topics.setIamPolicy , to grant the sink's writer identity publisher on the topic. roles/pubsub.editor does not cover it \u2014 without it the sink is created but silently publishes nothing. roles/run.admin deploying the adapter and setting its invoker policy roles/iam.serviceAccountAdmin the two service accounts the module creates PROJECT=your-project MEMBER=user:you@example.com for ROLE in logging.configWriter pubsub.admin run.admin iam.serviceAccountAdmin; do gcloud projects add-iam-policy-binding \"$PROJECT\" \\ --member=\"$MEMBER\" --role=\"roles/$ROLE\" done Check it directly rather than inferring from role names. There is no gcloud projects test-iam-permissions subcommand, so the REST call is the way \u2014 an empty permissions array back means none are granted: curl -s -X POST \\ -H \"Authorization: Bearer $(gcloud auth print-access-token)\" \\ -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\ -d '{\"permissions\":[ \"logging.sinks.create\",\"logging.sinks.update\", \"logging.exclusions.create\",\"logging.buckets.update\", \"pubsub.topics.setIamPolicy\" ]}' \\ \"https://cloudresourcemanager.googleapis.com/v1/projects/$PROJECT:testIamPermissions\" # APIs setup.sh checks these and offers to enable them: gcloud services enable \\ compute.googleapis.com run.googleapis.com pubsub.googleapis.com \\ logging.googleapis.com artifactregistry.googleapis.com iam.googleapis.com \\ --project=\"$PROJECT\" Only project-level IAM is visible locally; org-level constraints surface at apply time. The two most likely to bite are a constraint blocking external HTTP(S) load balancers, and constraints/iam.disableServiceAccountKeyCreation . <h2"},{"id":"terraform-trap","title":"The Terraform trap that fails silently","text":"id=\"terraform-trap\"> # The Terraform trap that fails silently Neither hashicorp/google nor google-beta (v7) models loggingHttpRequestHeaders \u2014 log_config exposes only enable , optional_fields , optional_mode and sample_rate . The module therefore shells out to gcloud from a terraform_data + local-exec . The allow-list survives Terraform only if it is re-asserted The first version keyed the shell-out on a hash of the inputs and paired it with a destroy-time provisioner that removed the list again. On the next full terraform apply that destroy provisioner fired during a replacement and wrote back a logConfig with no loggingHttpRequestHeaders . Logs kept flowing. They simply arrived with nothing to classify on, and terraform plan said \u201cNo changes\u201d because Terraform cannot see a field it does not model. The fix is two resources rather than one. terraform_data.request_logging triggers on timestamp() , so every apply re-installs the list and any drift heals itself. Removal moved to terraform_data.request_logging_cleanup , whose triggers never change, so it fires only on a genuine terraform destroy . The cost is one unavoidable change in every plan. Do not \u201cclean that up\u201d \u2014 the trade is an invisible failure of the pipeline's whole purpose against a noisy plan. <h2"},{"id":"gotchas","title":"Before you trust the numbers","text":"id=\"gotchas\"> # Before you trust the numbers Config changes take minutes to reach the edge After re-installing the allow-list, requests sent ~60 seconds later were still logged with no headers. The same probe at ~10 minutes carried all of them. Budget several minutes before trusting a run taken straight after an apply, and treat a sudden run of headerless events after a deploy as propagation rather than breakage. Excluding from _Default really does hide the logs Once exclude_from_default_bucket takes effect, gcloud logging read returns nothing for the load balancer \u2014 the entries are routed but never stored. That is the intended saving, and it means Pub/Sub is the only place to read raw entries . make sample reads storage, so it goes quiet once the exclusion lands; pull the verify subscription instead. Delivery is at-least-once A push that fails after the adapter already forwarded some of its events is retried whole. After max_delivery_attempts the message parks on the dead-letter topic, where it stays queued and replayable. The adapter image is built locally, not in Cloud Build terraform apply builds and pushes a container unless you set adapter_image . That needs docker and an authenticated gcloud on the machine running apply. In a default-configured project Cloud Build runs as the default compute service account, which cannot read its own staging bucket \u2014 a local build needs only a configured credential helper, and is faster besides. <h2"},{"id":"cost","title":"Cost","text":"id=\"cost\"> # Cost Per 1M requests/month, ~1 KB per log entry: Component Cost Pub/Sub ~$0.04 Cloud Run adapter $0 \u2014 free tier, scales to zero Cloud Logging routing $0 \u2014 routing to a sink is not charged Cloud Logging storage $0 with exclude_from_default_bucket Pipeline total under $1/month The load balancer itself is not free \u2014 roughly $18/month for the forwarding rule plus per-GB processing, whatever the traffic. That does not matter to a customer who already runs one; it matters a lot for a demo rig that exists only to make traffic. Confirm before quoting Check current forwarding-rule and data-processing pricing before quoting any of these numbers to anyone. <h2"},{"id":"teardown","title":"Tearing it down","text":"id=\"teardown\"> # Tearing it down make destroy # terraform destroy, then check nothing is left standing make leftovers # just tell me what is still deployed Do this between runs. The forwarding rule bills at idle whether or not traffic is flowing, and it is the single most expensive thing in the repo. terraform destroy needs project_id , which has no default. Keep it in site/terraform/terraform.tfvars ; the script checks for it up front rather than letting Terraform stall on a prompt. destroy.sh --sweep deletes leftovers rather than only reporting them \u2014 useful if a destroy half-finishes, since a Cloud Run service or backend service left behind will not show up in any later plan. <h2"},{"id":"unverified","title":"Still unverified","text":"id=\"unverified\"> # Still unverified Do not assert these: per-header value truncation in loggingHttpRequestHeaders \u2014 the count limit is settled at 10, and nothing in a 40-request run came back truncated whether request logging is sampled independently of sample_rate under load, the way CloudFront real-time logs turned out to be best-effort. Three runs of 40 delivered 39, 40 and 40 \u2014 no evidence of loss, far too small a sample to call it exact end-to-end latency beyond the observed ~30\u201360s"}]},{"href":"troubleshooting.html","title":"Troubleshooting","group":"Support","text":"Symptom, cause, fix for both clouds. Most reported bugs are documented behaviour of the underlying service. Troubleshooting Symptom, cause, fix. Most of what gets reported as a bug here is documented behaviour of the underlying service. <h2","sections":[{"id":"order","title":"Work down this list first","text":"id=\"order\"> # Work down this list first In order. Do not skip to Terraform. cd <cloud> && make test \u2014 does the adapter still produce a valid payload? Needs no cloud account. If this fails, nothing downstream matters. ./setup.sh <cloud> status \u2014 what is actually deployed, including resources that fell out of Terraform state. node obsero.mjs --token <id> --domain <host> \u2014 is the tracking ID accepted? A rejected token means the pipeline forwards into nothing, silently . make -C <cloud> logs \u2014 is the adapter being invoked, and what does it say? Then the tables below. <h2"},{"id":"either-cloud","title":"Either cloud","text":"id=\"either-cloud\"> # Either cloud Symptom Cause Fix Analytics shows one page per query string path is being sent with the query string on it Send the path only. buildEvent strips it for you; a custom parser must too. This is the most common mistake by far. Duplicate events Delivery is at-least-once on both clouds Expected. One failed event fails its whole batch, which is retried, duplicating what already landed. Nothing arrives and nothing errors Often a rejected tracking ID node obsero.mjs --token <id> --domain <host> . One second to rule out. setup.sh step 2 refuses to apply The tracking ID in terraform.tfvars is still the placeholder set-in-step-2 Run step 2 and enter a real ID, or ./setup.sh <cloud> pipeline --token <id> . sec-fetch-mode is cors , and connection: keep-alive appeared Node's fetch overrides both Harness artefact only. Every User-Agent and every other header passes through untouched. A resource survives terraform destroy Partial destroy, or lost state \u2014 neither shows up in a plan ./destroy.sh <cloud> --check reports them with the exact command to remove each; --sweep runs those commands. <h2"},{"id":"aws","title":"AWS","text":"id=\"aws\"> # AWS Symptom Cause Fix Nothing arrives, everything reports healthy, Kinesis IncomingRecords is 0 The LogDeliveryEnabled = true tag is missing from the Firehose stream (standard mode) The module applies it automatically. If someone hand-built the stream, add it. There is no error message for this anywhere . Nothing yet, and the deploy finished under a minute ago The 60\u201390s latency floor Wait. CloudFront flushes continuously but Firehose buffers for at least 60s, and that is not a knob. 10 of 30 requests missing, no pattern by status, path, client or latency (realtime) CloudFront delivers real-time logs best-effort Not a bug and not fixable from here. Treat counts as a high-fidelity sample, never a ledger. Do not reconcile against origin logs or bill on them. Every column in a parsed record is shifted (realtime) CloudFront emits fields in its own canonical order, ignoring the log config, with no header row The module sorts log_fields canonically and hands the same list to the adapter; log_fields_in_order exports it. Never trust the config order positionally. No signature-agent / signature-input / signature ever arrives log_source = \"standard\" . Standard logging v2 has no cs-headers field. Switch to realtime . Not a config tweak \u2014 a different log source, at a ~$11/mo floor. Adapter returns 429; events land in the backup bucket Lambda concurrency exhausted \u2014 new accounts cap at 10 account-wide, and at that ceiling reserved_concurrent_executions cannot be set at all Nothing is lost; replay the backup bucket. Watch Throttles on the adapter. terraform destroy refuses to delete a bucket It holds objects Terraform did not create \u2014 exactly what the backup bucket collects Use ./destroy.sh aws , which empties it first. The module keeps backup_force_destroy = false on purpose, so a real destroy stops rather than discarding events that never reached Obsero. Connect says could not list CloudFront distributions Your AWS identity lacks cloudfront:ListDistributions , or the CLI is not logged in Run aws cloudfront list-distributions yourself \u2014 its error names the missing permission. Connect lists nothing, or not the distribution you expected The CLI is pointed at a different account aws sts get-caller-identity shows which. Set AWS_PROFILE and re-run; distributions are global, so region does not matter here. Apply fails with a ConflictException on a delivery source The distribution already had a standard logging v2 source that was not passed in existing_delivery_sources Re-run ./setup.sh aws connect : it re-reads the sources and fills the variable in. In your own Terraform, add the source's name there. Only one source per distribution is allowed. Apply fails because a connected distribution does not exist It was deleted \u2014 typically the mock site, destroyed and redeployed with a new ID Steps 2 and 3 drop missing distributions automatically. By hand: ./setup.sh aws connect --dist none , then connect again. Connected, but nothing arrives from your own site No traffic yet, or it has not been 90 seconds Request a page, wait out the latency floor , then make -C aws logs . aws logs describe-deliveries --region us-east-1 confirms the delivery exists. The connect picker says it needs bash 4 macOS ships bash 3.2 brew install bash , then re-run. Or skip the picker: ./setup.sh aws connect --dist <id> still needs bash 4, so use the Terraform variables directly. A CloudFront distribution survives the destroy CloudFront needs two operations, minutes apart Disable it, wait for Deployed , then delete. destroy.sh reports it rather than pretending it can be done in one pass. <h2"},{"id":"gcp","title":"GCP","text":"id=\"gcp\"> # GCP Symptom Cause Fix There are no LB request logs at all The origin is a backend bucket . BackendBucket has no logConfig field in the Compute v1 API. The origin has to become a backend service \u2014 Cloud Run, GKE, a MIG. No config change fixes this. Events arrive with no headers to classify on, and terraform plan says \u201cNo changes\u201d The allow-list was wiped by a destroy-time provisioner during a replacement. Terraform cannot see a field it does not model. Already fixed as two resources: terraform_data.request_logging re-installs on every apply, ..._cleanup fires only on destroy. Do not remove the every-apply change to quiet the plan. Headerless events for a few minutes after an apply Config propagation to the edge Wait. Measured: headerless at ~60s, all headers present at ~10 minutes. gcloud logging read returns nothing for the load balancer exclude_from_default_bucket is working \u2014 entries are routed but never stored That is the intended saving. Pub/Sub is the only place to read raw entries. Pull the verify subscription instead of make sample . The sink exists but publishes nothing The writer identity lacks publisher on the topic Needs roles/pubsub.admin at apply time \u2014 pubsub.editor does not cover pubsub.topics.setIamPolicy . terraform apply fails at the sink roles/logging.configWriter missing Editor does not imply it. See the roles table \u2192 Adding an eleventh logged header is rejected The API caps loggingHttpRequestHeaders at 10 Decide which one to drop. user-agent , referer and the client IP arrive as first-class fields anyway, so never spend a slot on those. The container image build fails Missing docker , or an unconfigured credential helper The build is deliberately local \u2014 Cloud Build's default compute service account cannot read its own staging bucket in a default project. Or set adapter_image to skip the build. terraform destroy prompts for project_id It has no default Keep it in gcp/site/terraform/terraform.tfvars . destroy.sh checks for it up front. A service account survives --sweep Service account IDs cap at 30 characters, so the module truncates the prefix to 21 A name match on the full prefix misses them. destroy.sh already truncates the same way; pass --prefix if you renamed things. An external LB cannot be created at all Org policy Only project-level IAM is visible locally; org constraints surface at apply time. The other likely one is constraints/iam.disableServiceAccountKeyCreation . <h2"},{"id":"notes","title":"Where the measured behaviour lives","text":"id=\"notes\"> # Where the measured behaviour lives aws/NOTES.md and gcp/NOTES.md record behaviour observed on live deployments rather than inferred from docs. They are separate from the READMEs because none of it is needed to use the modules \u2014 only to trust their numbers, or to hand-build a pipeline yourself. If you measure something new, add it there as well as here."}]}];
